2026-06-17 feat/dim-team-competition-season-mapping — team↔competition↔season membership mapping dim (Phase 1 of the dim_team entity/affiliation split). CPO DIRECTIVES (this conversation, 2026-06-17), recorded here per the CPO's E1 ruling below that up-front design approvals are logged alongside review-time rulings. Impetus: the 2026-06-16 handover's dim_team BL1/BL2 watch-item. Read-only root-cause investigation established the cause is NOT an ingestion gap (raw HAS BL1/BL2 /teams) but a DESIGN issue: dim_team.league_code is a latest-ingest provenance stamp (one row per team, latest /teams payload wins), so all 19 BL1 + 19 BL2 clubs are present but stamped DFBP (German Cup /teams ingested 2026-06-16, after the leagues' 2026-06-10); latent for every league (PL survived only by ingest timing); the live preview is unaffected (reads league_code from fixtures, not the stamp).
  CPO DESIGN RULINGS (conversation, 2026-06-17 — up-front approvals, presented with options + recommendations, the CPO deciding each):
  (D1) FIX SHAPE = mirror the player entity/affiliation split (dim_player + dim_player_team_season_mapping): a new core relationship (mapping) dim for team↔competition↔season membership. Rejected the band-aid stamp-priority tiebreak and the accept-and-document options.
  (D2) NAME = dim_team_competition_season_mapping (CPO chose the _mapping suffix mirroring the player dim over the shorter dim_team_competition_season).
  (D3) MEMBERSHIP SOURCE = FIXTURES, not the /teams roster. Rationale the CPO surfaced: unlike a player (a squad member may never play, which forced the player roster source), a team in a competition always plays fixtures in it, so fixtures are a complete, timing-stable source — and this drops the /teams feed (the broken stamp's source) out of the affiliation path entirely.
  (D4) INCLUDE upcoming/scheduled fixtures (a team is a member from the moment its schedule is published).
  (D5) EXPLICIT thin dim in core (not derived inline at each consumer).
  (D6) STAMP DISPOSITION = DROP dim_team.league_code (mirror dim_player), NOT keep-and-rename — renaming still forces the one consumer repoint (mart_team_market_value's where league_code='WC') for no benefit and leaves a misuse-able column. (Phase 2, not this PR.)
  (D7) TWO PHASES. THIS PR = Phase 1: add the new mapping dim only (net-new, breaks nothing, makes BL1/BL2 correct). Phase 2 (separate PR) = drop dim_team.league_code + repoint mart_team_market_value + point the season-rollup at the new spine.
  Contract approved by the CPO before any code ("yes, as recommended"). Reviewers: scope-auditor + analytics-engineer-reviewer (all paths dbt_project/**). Full BQ build deferred to ci-data-build; local check_layer_contract + sqlfluff + dbt parse green; read-only BQ spot-check confirmed BL1/BL2 = 19/19, grain unique (0 dups), multi-comp clubs (Bayern/Dortmund) now under BL1+DFBP+UCL+CWC.
  REVIEW-CYCLE RULINGS (2026-06-17):
  - analytics-engineer-reviewer FAILed the first pass on two NON-§10 findings (builder fixed, re-reviewed → PASS): (a) the completeness test was near-circular → reframed as a bidirectional mapping⟺fct_fixture cross-model consistency guard (the join-path regression is already covered by the team_sk→dim_team relationships test); (b) grain robustness depended on an untested league_api_id↔league_code 1:1 assumption → restructured with a GROUP BY so the grain is enforced, not assumed. scope-auditor FAILed a doc-accuracy nit (core.yml SK description said team_id, should say team_sk) → fixed.
  - (E1 — §10 governance classification) scope-auditor ESCALATEd: the contract records up-front "CPO-approved" design decisions but no matching escalations.log entry existed; does an up-front design approval need logging, or is the contract's decisions_taken sufficient? Put to the CPO in plain language (two paths, no recommendation). CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-06-17): **Path A — LOG IT.** Up-front CPO design approvals are recorded in escalations.log alongside review-time rulings (this entry); the contract's decisions_taken is not, by itself, the sole record.

2026-06-17 feat/dim-team-pure-entity — Phase 2 of the dim_team entity/affiliation split (Phase 1 = PR #488, merged cf2a0be). Executes the D6/D7 up-front approvals logged above. CPO directed Phase 2 this conversation ("Phase 2", then "go ahead as recommended"). Scope (CPO-confirmed): (a) DROP dim_team.league_code → dim_team is now a pure entity (mirrors dim_player); (b) REPOINT mart_team_market_value off the stamp onto dim_team_competition_season_mapping (select distinct team_sk where league_code='WC'), output schema preserved (literal 'WC' as league_code, grain one-row-per-team_sk); (c) update core.yml dim_team schema + layering.md note. The repoint corrects the WC team set from the incomplete stamp (23 teams) to the full WC field (48) — the CPO was informed of the 23→48 change before approving; mart is NOT exported (no script/site ref) and has 0 rows with a market value (source = parked #476/#418), so structural-only, no live/shipped impact. DEFERRED (CPO: "keep Phase 2 to items 1-3"): the season-rollup enhancement (point mart_team_season / int_team_season at the spine to surface upcoming-only teams) → its own PR; base_apif__teams_global left untouched (its league_code becomes an unused passenger). Reviewers: scope-auditor + analytics-engineer-reviewer BOTH PASS, NO §10 escalation — both confirmed zero dangling dim_team.league_code references across all 19 consumers (every league_code they carry comes from a fact/intermediate CTE) and grain preserved; the 23→48 structural change was CPO-pre-approved. Local check_layer_contract + sqlfluff + dbt parse green; full BQ build to ci-data-build.

2026-06-18 chore/dbt-mcp-server — CPO §10 CLASSIFICATION ruling + wiring of a read-only dbt MCP server. Impetus: a LinkedIn post on dbt Labs' "Wizard" prompted evaluating whether a metadata/lineage-aware agent helps us. Builder evaluation (this conversation): most of what the post sells we already have (validate-local + ci-data-build + DQ + the reviewer cycle = the "validation loop"; our own skills beat generic dbt skills given the zero-file/league_code architecture). The one genuine gap = edit-time lineage/impact-analysis; the dbt MCP server's local-manifest tools close it. Read-only probe (no warehouse, no cost): dbt-mcp shells out to our dbt 1.7.19 (NO dbt-core dependency -> NO forced dbt upgrade, the feared risk); dbt-artifacts-parser 0.13.2 parses our v11 manifest cleanly; dbt-mcp 1.20.4 needs Python >=3.12 (run via a uv-managed interpreter; our venv stays 3.11.9). The full hosted "metadata engine" (Discovery: model_health/run_results/performance) is dbt-Cloud-gated -> NOT available to us; we get local lineage + node details only.
  (Q - §10 classification, put to the CPO in plain language with two paths) Is `.mcp.json` (a file that auto-launches `uvx dbt-mcp` every session) a PROTECTED command-class path, or an ordinary scoped file? The meta-rule (working_agreement §213: novel-case classification is a CPO decision; "it's analogous to X" is not a license) forbids the builder deciding it. Path 1 = ordinary (small PR, scope-auditor only; leaves a hole - a future agent could self-grant any MCP server with only scope-auditor review). Path 2 = PROTECTED command-class (governance task; extends the guard list + routes to cto-reviewer/opus; closes the hole). The CPO asked for a recommendation; builder recommended Path 2 (consistency with the 2026-06-14 `.claude/commands/` ruling - both auto-run a command; lopsided cost; cheapest to set the rule at the first MCP server).
      CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-06-18): **Path 2 - PROTECTED (command-class).** Authorizes the protected_override edits to .claude/hooks/task_contract_gate.py (add .mcp.json + .cursor/mcp.json to PROTECTED_FILES) and .claude/review_routing.json (route both -> cto-reviewer + opus floor), synced into working_agreement.md §2 + agent_guardrails.md, AND wiring the read-only server (.mcp.json: uvx --python 3.12.13 dbt-mcp, allowlist DBT_MCP_ENABLE_TOOLS=get_lineage_dev,get_node_details_dev,list,parse - verified live to expose exactly those 4 tools, zero warehouse tools, and to return real lineage for dim_date via get_lineage_dev).
  Two builder judgment calls surfaced at contract time + CPO-approved ("Approved"): (a) protect BOTH .mcp.json (Claude Code) and .cursor/mcp.json (Cursor) - protecting only the literal file would leave the Cursor entry point open and half-defeat the ruling's goal; (b) do NOT add the redundant mcp__dbt__* denylist to the PROTECTED .claude/settings.json - the allowlist already makes those tools non-existent, so it would be a second protected edit for no gain. DEFERRED: creating .cursor/mcp.json (Cursor's own config) - this PR protects the path but only creates the Claude Code .mcp.json. Reviewers: scope-auditor + cto-reviewer (opus, guard-path floor).

